Kg1 h4 59. Kh1 h3 60. Ra1 Kg5 61. Rb1 Re2 62. Ra1 Rg2 $4 {The play, which has
progressed steadily during the week, has attracted a large number of
spectators, and has been marked by unusual and even extraordinary incidents.
In the third round, much to the amusement of the bystanders, von Scheve drew
an apparently hopeless game with Lee. In the following position:—[#] For his
last move Lee had played R from K 7 to K Kt 7, von Scheve checked with his
Rook, and then by repeatedly offering to sacrifice it, forced a stale-mate. 
(BCM, September 1890, p364)} 63. Ra5+ Kf4 {Sources: Manchester Guardian, 27
August 1890; BCM, October 1890, p427, though one imagines that Black would
have waited to see 64 Rf5+ on the board before agreeing a draw.} (63... Kf4 64.
Rf5+ {Source: A Selection of Games from the International Tournament played at
Manchester 1890 (1891), p17}) 1/2-1/2

