1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. Qd2 {
Blackburne's own invention, frequently played by him in his match with Lee.} a6
8. Nd1 c5 9. c3 Nc6 10. f4 cxd4 11. cxd4 Qb4 {Exhibiting a plentiful lack of
pluck. As a well-known master observes - "This may be lawn tennis or marbles,
but it certainly is not Chess."} 12. Nf3 Qxd2+ 13. Kxd2 b6 14. Ne3 Bb7 15. Be2
f5 16. Rhg1 O-O {Very bad, and quite contrary to common sense, to play his
king in the line of fire.} 17. g4 fxg4 18. Rxg4 Rf7 19. Bd3 Nf8 20. f5 {
Blackburne now rapidly brings matters to a crisis.} exf5 21. Nxf5 Rc7 {And
Black shuffles about until the preparations for his execution are completed.}
22. Rag1 Ne6 23. Nd6 Ra7 24. Rh4 g6 25. Bxg6 {More Blackburneico.} hxg6 26.
Rxg6+ Ng7 27. Rgh6 {This is becoming solemn. Tinsley might gracefully give in.}
Kf8 28. Rh8+ Ke7 29. R4h7 Nxe5 30. dxe5 Ke6 31. Rh6+ Kd7 32. Nd4 {[#]BCM,
September 1890, p365, which doesn't give the moves of the game, nevertheless
gives a position which approximates to this in order to showcase the final
combination but with several differences - no pawns on a6, b6, a2, b2 or h2,
but with WP on a3 and f3 and the WK on g2. However, they later gave the
complete score with notes by Tinsley. Here Tinsley himself comments: "After
this Black might well have surrendered, as there is no loophole of escape. By
playing to the end, however, he allows the great master to perform prettily;
or to use the words of a friendly (?) critic, 'has the advantage of being
mated in elegant style.'" - JS} Ra8 33. R6h7 Rxh8 34. Rxg7+ Kd8 35. Ne6# {
"A very pleasing and characteristically Blackburneian dénouement." Sources:
The Chess Player's Chronicle 1889/90, p.213-214; Birmingham Daily Gazette;
Bristol Times and Mirror - Saturday 20 September 1890; BCM, November 1890, p458
} 1-0

