{"The first half-hour’s play in the fourth round commenced at noon to-day in
the Athenaeum Lecture Hall was marked by a sensational incident. Tarrasch and
Gunsberg were paired with each other, and on account of the emi­nence of the
players many of the spectators concentrated their attention on this game,
which promised to be a pro­longed and interesting struggle. In less than half
an hour, however, there came a sudden collapse. On his eleventh move Gunsberg
fell into a trap, and finding that the mis­hap would cost him a piece, he at
once resigned. It was stated that Tarrasch had successfully laid the same trap
for Zukertort in the Frankfort tournament. The following are the moves of what
will perhaps be known in future as “Tarrasch’s trap”" (Manchester
Evening News, 27 August 1890):—} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 (3... a6 4.
Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. c3 Be7 10. Re1 O-O 11.
Nd4 Qd7 $4 12. Nxe6 fxe6 13. Rxe4 {Source: Tarrasch: Dreihundert Schachpartien
(1925), p279}) 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 a6 6. Ba4 {(Recorded as 6 B-B4 in the
Manchester Evening News on the day the game was played, and also given in some
subsequent newspaper reports - but it seems improbable - JS)} b5 7. Bb3 d5 8.
dxe5 Be6 9. c3 Be7 10. Re1 O-O 11. Nd4 Qd7 $4 12. Nxe6 {Sources: Manchester
Evening News - Wednesday 27 August 1890; Manchester Guardian, 28 August 1890;
Morning Post - Thursday 28 August 1890; Manchester Times - Friday 29 August
1890; Evening News (London) - Friday 29 August 1890; Newcastle Chronicle -
Saturday 30 August 1890; BCM, September 1890, p365; Illustrated Sporting and
Dramatic News - Saturday 20 September 1890. Two days later D Mills caught his
minor tournament opponent, W Schott, in exactly the same trap!} 1-0

