1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 {Perhaps on the whole this defence must be considered as
inferior to the “French,” which in its leading features it so closely
resembles, notwithstanding the narrower range of attack it affords the
opponent at the outset.} 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 Be7 ({Though} 
6... Bd6 {has its drawbacks, it seems a better move than this.}) 7. O-O O-O 8.
Re1 ({Stronger far than the old} 8. c4 {, the ultimate effect of which should
be to leave White with a weak Pawn at d4. The Black Knight must now retreat,
as if it be supported by 8...f5, or 8...Bf5, White may continue with 9 c4 with
advantage.}) 8... Nf6 9. Bf4 {A troublesome move for Black, and doubtless the
best one for White just here.} Bg4 ({Weak, as the consequent exchange proves
very unfavourable.} 9... Nc6 {would have been much better.}) 10. Nbd2 Nh5 {
And the same may be said of this. The time lost by the second player,
hereabouts, compromises his game almost beyond recovery.} 11. Be3 Nc6 12. h3 {
Naturally! Should Black now retire his Bishop to e6, White, by 13 Ng5, would
have decidedly the best of it. Yet even this would have been a lesser evil
than the capture; and the following 13...g6, in support of the threatened
Knight, which of course ought to have been brought back to f3.} Bxf3 13. Qxf3
g6 14. c3 Qd7 {This turns out rather worse than useless, though its purport
was to defend the Queen’s Pawn against the adversary’s contemplated double
attack upon it, through Bh6, Nf1, and Ne3.} 15. Nf1 Nd8 16. Bh6 Ng7 (16... Re8
{, though attended with considerable danger, would have been more to the
purpose. Taken altogether Black’s defence is very poor, though perhaps with
the best play his difficulties were not to be surmounted.}) 17. Ne3 c6 18. Ng4
Nde6 19. Re2 Qd8 20. Rae1 {The Dr. knows well how to bide his time! In thus
stopping to double his Rooks before otherwise further proceeding, he loses
nothing, and gains much.} Bg5 ({This proves fatal, leading as it does to
direct loss of material, and a yet more deteriorated position.} 20... Re8 {
would have been much better; and, if then,} 21. Ne5 {,} Bf6 {might have still
left him something to play for.}) 21. Bxg5 Nxg5 22. Qf6 {Forcing the exchange
of Queens, and the surest if not shortest way of winning.} Qxf6 ({There was
nothing else. Of course, if} 22... N5e6 23. Nh6+ {, followed by 24 Nxf7+, and
25 Nxd8 would leave White a piece and a Pawn to the good.}) 23. Nxf6+ Kh8 24.
Re7 {With the unquestioned establishment of the Rook on the seventh rank,
White’s victory becomes merely a matter of time, and the rest is but a
struggle on Black’s part to stave off the evil moment as long as possible.}
N5e6 {There was no means of preventing the loss of Pawns following on this, as
White threatened to double his Rooks on the seventh rank, in which event
Black’s position must have become hopeless.} 25. Rxb7 Rab8 26. Rxa7 Rxb2 27.
Nd7 {The ensuing manoeuvres of this Knight are highly instructive—and
greatly destructive also.} Rc8 28. Ne5 Rbb8 {White threatened 29 Nxc6 among
other things.} 29. Nxf7+ Kg8 30. Nh6+ Kh8 31. Nf7+ Kg8 32. Nh6+ ({JS note: The
imminent (and improbable) threefold repetition in Mason's BCM annotation
doesn't appear in other versions of the score, which proceed} 32. Ne5 Ra8 33.
Rxa8 Rxa8 34. Nxc6 Rxa2 35. Ne7+ Kf8 ({Another version of this score ends} 
35... Kf7 36. Nxd5 Ra3 37. Bc4 Kf8 38. Nb6 Kf7 39. d5 {1-0 - Deutsches
Wochenschach, Vol.6 no.48, 30 November 1890, ppn 400-403, "comments from Dr. S.
Tarrasch according to Deutschen Schachzug"}) 36. Nxd5 Ra3 37. Bc4 {("Resigns"
International Chess Magazine)} h6 38. Nb6 {Sources: Tarrasch: Dreihundert
Schachpartien (1925), p284-288; ICM (Steinitz) 1890, p309; The Chess Monthly
1890/1, p146. The Deutsche Schachzeitung, November 1890, pages 338-341,
continues:} Kf7 39. d5 {resigns. Edward Winter comments on the different score
versions in his Tarrasch article: https://www.chesshistory.com/winter/extra/
tarrasch.html}) 32... Kh8 33. Nf7+ Kg8 34. Ne5 Ra8 35. Rxa8 Rxa8 36. Nxc6 Rxa2
37. Ne7+ Kf8 38. Nxd5 Ra3 39. Bc4 h6 40. Nb6 {"The game was adjourned at this
point, pro forma; but Black resigned without resuming, as his position was
manifestly hopeless."  Source (for this specific version of the score - for
others, see above): BCM, October 1890, p433, with Mason's annotations. However,
the game is headed "A friendly contest, played August 28th, at Manchester."
This was the day before they met in the tournament proper in Manchester, yet
this score has become the de facto record of their formal 8th round game. To
add to the confusion, the Manchester Courier for Saturday 30 August stated
that the game was adjourned on the 42nd move. All very strange!} 1-0

