1. d4 f5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 e6 4. Nh3 d5 5. O-O Be7 6. Nd2 O-O 7. c3 Qe8 8. Re1
c5 9. e3 Nc6 10. f3 b6 11. Nf2 Bb7 12. Nb3 Nd8 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. e4 fxe4 15.
fxe4 Rc8 16. exd5 Bxd5 17. Bxd5 Nxd5 18. Nd2 Qf7 19. Nde4 Nb7 20. Nd3 c4 21.
Nf4 Nc5 22. Nxc5 Bxc5+ 23. Kg2 Qb7 24. Kh3 Rf6 25. Re5 Rd8 26. Nxd5 Rxd5 27.
Rxd5 exd5 28. Bf4 Bd6 29. Be3 Qf7 30. Kg2 Qe6 31. Qd4 Qf5 32. Rd1 Qf3+ 33. Kg1
Bc5 {As the "G.O.M." of Chess lifted his gouty hand to make this move, his
face was a study for a painter. The rest is... not worthy of publication. Of
course if Qxc5, White is mated in two by Qxd1+ and Qf1. Our great English
champion played this game feebly, and in a way to assist the attacking
propensities of his opponent. (Tinsley, BCM)} 34. Qxd5+ Qxd5 35. Rxd5 Bxe3+ 36.
Kg2 Rf2+ 37. Kh3 Rxb2 38. Rd8+ Kf7 39. Rd7+ Kf6 40. Rc7 Bg1 41. Rxc4 Rxa2 42.
Rf4+ Ke6 43. Re4+ Kd6 44. Kg4 Rxh2 45. c4 a5 46. Re1 Bd4 {Sources: Manchester
Evening News, 5 September 1890; Manchester Guardian, 5 September 1890; Eastern
Daily Press - Tuesday 16 September 1890; BCM, October 1890, p423; Deutsches
Wochenschach, Vol.6 no.42, 19 October 1890, p354; A Selection of Games from
the International Tournament played at Manchester 1890 (1891), p. 9-10;
Blackburne Games by Tim Harding (McFarland), p302} 0-1

