1. Nf3 d5 2. d4 e6 ({Tchigorin played} 2... Bg4 {in this positiion several
times in his Match with Steinitz in Havana, but lost on most occasions. Mr.
Blackburne also played it twice in the late Blackburne v Lee match, winning in
one case, and losing in the other. The German school however, prefer the text
move.}) 3. Bf4 {Played with much success by. J.Mason.} (3. e3) ({or} 3. c4 {
is more generally adopted here.}) 3... c5 4. Bxb8 Rxb8 5. e3 c4 6. Be2 (6. b3 {
is better here. If Black replays with} b5 {, than} 7. a4 {with a good game.})
6... Bd6 7. c3 b5 8. Nbd2 Nf6 9. Qc2 O-O 10. Ne5 {A blunder, which loses a
pawn, or exposes Withe's game.} (10. O-O) ({or} 10. b4 {was better.}) 10...
Bxe5 11. dxe5 Nd7 12. f4 {White plays this move as the only means of
protecting the pawn at e5.} ({If White plays} 12. Nf3 {, Black replies with}
Qc7 {and wins the King's pawn.} {[Houdini 1.5:} 13. Ng5 g6 14. f4 b4 15. Nf3
bxc3 16. Qxc3 Nc5 $15 {]}) 12... Qb6 13. O-O ({White can defend the Pawn by} 
13. Nf1) ({or} 13. Kf2 {, but in each case Black proceeds with} f6 {with an
overwhelming attack. The text move holds out prospects.}) 13... Qxe3+ 14. Rf2
f6 15. Nf3 fxe5 16. Ng5 Nf6 17. Kf1 {In order to free the King's rook for
action. Black cannot now prevent White from playing Rf3 with attacking chances.
} h6 18. Rf3 Qc5 19. fxe5 hxg5 20. exf6 g4 21. Qg6 Rb7 {Best. At the same time
defending the threatend mate and bringing the inactive rook into powerful play.
} 22. b4 {Not so good as 22.Qxg4 at once. The idea is to strengthen the
Queen's side for the endgame. If Black replies 22...cxb3 en passant, then 23.
Bd3 with attacking chances. The text move is, however, bad, as it drives the
adverse queen into a better position.} Qd6 23. Qxg4 e5 24. Qg5 e4 25. Rf2 Rbf7
({Black cannot with advantage capture the h-pawn,} 25... Qxh2 {, on account of
White's reply} 26. Qxd5+ {followed by 27.Qh5, offering an exchange of queens
and leaving Black's pawns weak for the endgame.}) 26. Kg1 Rxf6 27. Rxf6 Rxf6
28. Rd1 Rf5 29. Qe3 Rf7 30. Qxe4 {An ingenoius device, which gives White a
drawn game. Black must capture the h-pawn, checking, to equalise matters, and
then, although a pawn ahead, cannot win, as the sequel shows.} Qxh2+ 31. Kxh2
dxe4 32. Rd8+ Rf8 33. Rxf8+ Kxf8 34. Kg3 g5 35. Kf2 Ke7 36. Ke3 Kd6 37. Kd4 e3
{By this move Black secures the draw.} 38. Kxe3 Kd5 39. g3 {Sources: The Chess
Player's Chronicle 1889/90, 20 September, p199; Eastern Daily Press - Tuesday
16 September 1890} 1/2-1/2

