Ng3 Bg6 6. Nf3 Nf6 7. Ne5 e6 8. c3 Nbd7 9. Nxg6 hxg6 10. Bd3 Bd6 11. Qf3 Qc7
12. Bd2 O-O-O 13. b4 e5 14. O-O e4 15. Bxe4 Nxe4 16. Qxe4 Nf6 17. Qf3 Nh5 18.
a4 f5 19. b5 Rh7 20. bxc6 bxc6 21. Rab1 Rdh8 22. Qd3 Nxg3 23. Qa6+ Kd8 24. fxg3
Rxh2 25. Bg5+ Ke8 26. Rfe1+ Kf8 27. Rb7 Bxg3 28. Be7+ Qxe7 29. Rb8+ Bxb8 30.
Rxe7 Kxe7 31. Qxc6 Rh1+ 32. Kf2 Kf7 33. d5 Re8 34. d6 Rd8 35. Qb7+ Kf8 $4 ({
There seems to be a score problem here as the text move loses instantly to 36
Qe7+ and it's hard to imagine that either player could have missed such an
obvious move. However, the only winning alternative,} 35... Kg8 {, runs into
problems if we assume that subsequent moves are recorded correctly, e.g.} 36.
d7 Rd1 {and now White would surely have played} 37. Qb3+ {winning the d1 rook
- JS}) 36. d7 Rd1 37. Qb4+ Bd6 38. Qb3 Bc5+ 39. Kf3 R8xd7 40. Kf4 Be7 41. g4
fxg4 42. Kxg4 {... newspaper report ('Manchester 1890' by AJ Gillam, The Chess
Player 2011)} 0-1

