1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 a6 6. Ba4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5
Be6 9. c3 Be7 10. Re1 O-O 11. Nd4 Qd7 $4 12. Nxe6 {Source: International Chess
Magazine, 1890, p278. This replicated the trap in which Tarrasch had caught
Gunsberg two days earlier in the Masters tournament. "The minor tournament is
exciting increased atten­tion among the visitors, as the crowds round the
boards last evening testified. Mr. Schott, the only competitor with a clean
scare, was paired with Mr. Mills. No little merriment was caused when it
became known that Mr. Mills had set “Tarrasch’s Trap” for his opponent.
All were watching in breathless silence to see if Mr. Schott would fall in,
and much to the surprise of everyone he fell a victim."} 1-0

